.TH CHESSCAN 1 "November 2026" "chesscan 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
chesscan \- live screen chess scanner with engine evaluation HUD
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chesscan
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chesscan
captures a rectangle of the screen containing a chess board, recognises the
pieces, converts the position to FEN, queries a UCI engine, and displays the
best lines with an evaluation and win probability in an always-on-top panel.
.PP
Board recognition is theme agnostic. Each square's background colour is
estimated locally, pixels far from it are treated as ink, and the resulting
silhouette plus ink colour forms a descriptor. The 64 descriptors are clustered
once during calibration and labelled by the user; the learned prototypes are
stored in
.I chesscan.json
and reused afterwards.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-detect
Locate the board automatically from its grid lines, save the rect, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-auto
Re-detect the board at startup, and again if reads start failing (for example
after the window was moved or resized).
.TP
.BI \-\-rect " x,y,w,h"
Set the board rectangle directly, bypassing detection.
.TP
.B \-\-flip
The board is drawn with black at the bottom.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Report session type, monitors, a capture attempt and board detection result, then
exit. First thing to run when capture fails.
.TP
.B \-\-calibrate
Cluster the current board and prompt for a FEN piece character per cluster.
Required once per theme; descriptors are scale-normalised, so resizing the board
alone does not require recalibration.
.TP
.B \-\-labels " c1,c2,..."
Non-interactive cluster labels for calibration, in cluster order.
.TP
.B \-\-snapshot
Print the detected board and FEN, then exit. No engine, no HUD.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-squares
Print one line per square: ink coverage, ink colour, and the nearest prototype
with its distance. Use it to tell a bad board rectangle from a bad classifier.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " PATH"
Read a saved screenshot instead of the live screen. Useful for testing and for
Wayland sessions where capture is unreliable.
.TP
.BI \-\-monitor " N"
Monitor index to capture. Default 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-engine " PATH"
UCI engine executable. Default
.IR stockfish .
.TP
.BI \-\-depth " N"
Search depth. Default 18.
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " MS"
Scan cadence in milliseconds. Default 900.
.TP
.BR \-\-white ", " \-\-black
Which side is to move at startup. Board changes toggle the side from there.
.TP
.BI \-\-classes " N"
Number of clusters to learn during calibration. Default 13.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-hud
Terminal output only.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/chesscan/config.json
Board rectangle, engine settings, and learned piece prototypes.
.B CHESSCAN_CONFIG
overrides the path; an existing
.I ./chesscan.json
from older setups is still honoured.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the board and learn the theme:
.PP
.nf
  chesscan --auto --calibrate
.fi
.PP
Then run:
.PP
.nf
  chesscan
.fi
.PP
Check recognition from a screenshot:
.PP
.nf
  chesscan --file board.png --snapshot
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Live screen capture uses X11. On a Wayland session the capture backend cannot
read the screen and reports "screen capture failed"; log in with "GNOME on Xorg"
or use
.B \-\-file
with a screenshot. Run
.B chesscan \-\-doctor
to confirm which session you are in.
.PP
Side to move is inferred by toggling on each detected board change rather than
read from the screen; start it correctly with
.B \-\-white
or
.B \-\-black .
Castling rights are inferred from king and rook placement. En passant and move
history cannot be recovered from pixels.
.PP
The HUD is deliberately opaque. Per-pixel alpha arrows drawn over the board
require a compositor ARGB path and are not implemented.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stockfish (6)